# Third-party sources

This file is the single record of content taken from another repository. `LICENSE` covers
only work authored here and points here; the root README links here instead of naming any
source. Add an entry when code, prompt text, or other content is taken from another
repository, and do not restate an entry anywhere else.

Fields for each entry: source repository URL, license, copyright holder, version observed,
where the content is used in this repository, what was changed, and that source's copyright
and permission notice. A field that cannot be established from evidence in this repository
(package metadata, a fetched source, git history, or existing text here) is recorded as unknown
with the evidence available; do not guess a license.

## pi-subagents

- Source repository: https://github.com/nicobailon/pi-subagents
- License: MIT
- Copyright holder: Copyright (c) 2026 Nico Bailon
- Version observed: 0.90.0, read from package.json of the package installed at
  `~/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/pi-subagents` on 2026-11-25 (0.89.2 on 2026-11-22). Nothing here
  pins that version; generation output depends on what is installed on the machine running it.
- Where the content is used: reference only — no prompt body from this source is committed.
  The install-path form appears as a comment line in `configs/permissions.example.src.jsonc:296`, as
  example paths in `README.md:39` and in `extensions/permission-mode-generator/README.md:31` and :80,
  where docs describe resolving a reference against an installed package. When a user points a
  reference at their own install, that file's content enters generated output at generation time under
  this license.
- Connection facts (no content taken): the permission-mode generator writes the `subagents` key of
  `~/.pi/agent/settings.json` and selected keys of `~/.pi/agent/extensions/subagent/config.json`,
  which the installed package reads (settings during agent discovery, refreshed on `session_start`;
  config through `loadConfig()` and its extension factory). Agent discovery and its priority are
  defined by the installed package. `lib/settings-lock.ts` takes a lock compatible with the
  package's physical-target `settings.json.write-lock` lease, alongside Pi's own settings lock;
  the package's config.json update code takes no lock. Nothing is copied: the lock protocol is
  implemented here from its observed behavior. Behavior can change with the installed version.
- What was changed: nothing from this source. Our contribution next to it is original:
  `configs/prompts/reviewer-addendum.md`, which states precedence over conflicting statements
  above it.
